f  screwing up or of having done something  really bad and then just left it for  years and years and years and years and  not done anything about that  getting forgiven and redeemed on the  back of as you know a single big gesture  and to be fair that's not entirely what  this episode is doing but there was  something about Ryan saying I forgive  you in in that moment that rang really  hollow to me because I don't think Ryan  would have said that under any of the  circumstances and I don't think it's  just because he was too better to say  what he felt I think he genuinely was  not ready to forgive him and the only  reason he did is because he thought he  was gonna die and the fact that that  kind of gets played for  like the genuine article that kind of  bothers me and I was worried about this  character from the get-go because there  was a line he had when they're in the  coffee shop and he says what do you want  me to say whatever it is I'll say it and  Ryan's response to that was pretty good  but the incident his dad said that I'm  like because it it just shows such a  fundamental misunderstanding of the  damage that you've done when you think  that there's some magic phrase or some  single gesture that you can do that's  gonna make it okay it's like no you need  to be prepared to spend as long as much  time as you spent getting it wrong you  need to be prepared to have to spend  that much time continuously doing it  right before you really have any  business asking or even implying  forgiveness you know a desire for  forgiveness and the tendency in  narratives to you know have it be big  gestures big speeches the right words  extenuating circumstances granting  people forgiveness is something that I  just I'm not gonna I'm not gonna say can  ever work and that it's never justified  it's just something that I see a lot and  doesn't sit right with me and this is  far from the most egregious example of  this kind of thing but it is a thing  that happens a lot and